ly. attavo build runs it each iteration, after the claims boundary, the criteria, and the selected tests -- pass requires its exit to be 0, and a non-zero exit reaches the executor as feedback. Optional; a tenant that declares none is not refused (unlike [tests], which build requires).\",\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/224/stub/schema/manifest.schema.json:76:      \"description\": \"The tenant's own definition of done: a whole-tree check, as the tenant runs it locally. attavo build runs it each iteration, after the claims boundary, the criteria, and the selected tests -- pass requires its exit to be 0, and a non-zero exit reaches the executor as feedback. Optional; a tenant that declares none is not refused (unlike [tests], which build requires).\",\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/264/stub/attavo/cli.py:25:The doctor is the definition of done. It asserts five proofs against a\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/264/stub/attavo/cli.py:250:(instruction, mechanism, guard, each a path and symbol or a ruling id),\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/records/153/counsel-01.json:46:    \"ruling_text\": \"The null question is not a question and changes no definition of done. The adapter correctly rejected it, leaving a no-cause executor stop. Retry the build from the existing worktree, complete the claimed ledger implementation, and conclude only after the criteria and required checks finish; an unfinished background test run is not grounds to halt.\",\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/records/219/counsel-02.json:46:    \"ruling_text\": \"Leave build-02.started for the driver to recover in its networked environment. Recovery of a dead invocation is explicitly the next pass's act; the recovered attempt counts against the stage budget, and the following pass retries it automatically as `died` when budget remains. Do not expand item 219 into investigating the test invocation or lease rollback: that is separate from this item's definition of done and its five acceptance criteria already pass. The failed local push does not authorize bypassing the lease or manually manufacturing the recovery transport.\",\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/records/219/verify-01.json:76:      \"retires\": \"Automatic reruns after a stop whose answer may alter or interpret the definition of done.\",\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/records/219/counsel-01.json:46:    \"ruling_text\": \"Continue the build from the existing working tree. The stop was a progress report, not a question requiring any change to the definition of done. Do not start or wait for a whole-suite or whole-tree run: the log already records all 11 retry tests passing and mypy succeeding with no type ignores. Review the current diff, run only the prescribed selected-test command if further validation is needed, resolve any concrete selected-test failure, and commit the completed implementation. Return status question only if an unresolved decision would change the criteria.\",\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/158/stub/schema/manifest.schema.json:81:      \"description\": \"The tenant's own definition of done: a whole-tree check, as the tenant runs it locally. attavo build runs it each iteration, after the claims boundary, the criteria, and the selected tests -- pass requires its exit to be 0, and a non-zero exit reaches the executor as feedback. Optional; a tenant that declares none is not refused (unlike [tests], which build requires).\",\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/229/spec.md:23:Retires: the working tree as what the build's gate reads; `pass` as a status a build with no commit can hold; a review of a build that changed nothing; a definition of done with no word about whether anything was built.\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/219/spec.md:3:After R2 a pass starts a stage and a worker runs it, but nothing in the product starts a stage again. A build whose worker died is recovered as `died` and then waits for a person; a review the vendor refused at capacity is a `failed` record, a counsel run, a filed issue, and two presses (#194's `verify-01`); a build whose head a required check rejected is a level (`blocked-on-checks`) that never becomes a record, so the item sits until someone reads the screen (#206's `build-01`, red on `typecheck`); a review that returns blocking findings stops the item as if the findings were a question for the operator. Every one of these is a failure whose answer does not touch the definition of done, and the project's rule is that such a failure needs the operator only if the spec must change. Nothing bounds a stage's wall clock either: a review on an adapter that streams no events is judged by a stall bound it can never trip, and the only ceiling is the operator's patience.\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/records/229/counsel-05.json:46:    \"ruling_text\": \"The Claims list is exhaustive. `attavo/common.py` is not claimed, so the build correctly refuses a commit that changes it. Implement the empty-build comparison within the claimed files\\u2014one claimed module may expose the shared predicate for the other claimed modules to import\\u2014and leave `attavo/common.py` unchanged. This does not alter the definition of done: the comparison remains against the approval tree, and a retried attempt may adopt valid prior-attempt commits.\",\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/212/stub/attavo/cli.py:18:The doctor is the definition of done. It asserts five proofs against a\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/212/stub/attavo/cli.py:145:(instruction, mechanism, guard, each a path and symbol or a ruling id),\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/242/stub/schema/manifest.schema.json:76:      \"description\": \"The tenant's own definition of done: a whole-tree check, as the tenant runs it locally. attavo build runs it each iteration, after the claims boundary, the criteria, and the selected tests -- pass requires its exit to be 0, and a non-zero exit reaches the executor as feedback. Optional; a tenant that declares none is not refused (unlike [tests], which build requires).\",\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/229/stub/attavo/stops.py:166:        provenance -- a question is about the definition of done, which is\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/212/stub/schema/manifest.schema.json:71:      \"description\": \"The tenant's own definition of done: a whole-tree check, as the tenant runs it locally. attavo build runs it each iteration, after the claims boundary, the criteria, and the selected tests -- pass requires its exit to be 0, and a non-zero exit reaches the executor as feedback. Optional; a tenant that declares none is not refused (unlike [tests], which build requires).\",\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/229/stub/tests/test_build.py:138:    not the definition of done the spec's claims describe). Tenant\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/229/stub/tests/test_build.py:157:    the definition of done, not a claim the build itself may touch.\"\"\"\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/229/stub/tests/test_build.py:645:    is about the definition of done, not the product's own mechanism.\"\"\"\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/229/stub/schema/manifest.schema.json:76:      \"description\": \"The tenant's own definition of done: a whole-tree check, as the tenant runs it locally. attavo build runs it each iteration, after the claims boundary, the criteria, and the selected tests -- pass requires its exit to be 0, and a non-zero exit reaches the executor as feedback. Optional; a tenant that declares none is not refused (unlike [tests], which build requires).\",\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/219/stub/schema/manifest.schema.json:71:      \"description\": \"The tenant's own definition of done: a whole-tree check, as the tenant runs it locally. attavo build runs it each iteration, after the claims boundary, the criteria, and the selected tests -- pass requires its exit to be 0, and a non-zero exit reaches the executor as feedback. Optional; a tenant that declares none is not refused (unlike [tests], which build requires).\",\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/records/151/counsel-03.json:46:    \"ruling_text\": \"The stop is non-question: the executor used the question channel with the placeholder text only to wait for its background confirmation run. That run was progress state, not a question about the definition of done; the question guard correctly rejects the short placeholder rather than sending it to a person.\",\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/229/stub/tests/test_doctor.py:4:fail at the first commit and must fail: that is the definition of done,\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/219/stub/attavo/stops.py:161:        provenance -- a question is about the definition of done, which is\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/records/151/counsel-01.json:46:    \"ruling_text\": \"The stop is non-question: the executor ended the build through the question channel with a null question. A background test still running is progress state, not a question about the definition of done. The adapter\\u2019s question guard governs this specimen; the stage should be recorded as non-question with executor provenance.\",\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/227/stub/tests/test_build.py:139:    not the definition of done the spec's claims describe). Tenant\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/227/stub/tests/test_build.py:158:    the definition of done, not a claim the build itself may touch.\"\"\"\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/227/stub/tests/test_build.py:646:    is about the definition of done, not the product's own mechanism.\"\"\"\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/227/stub/tests/test_counsel.py:291:    assert {c[\"kind\"] for c in record[\"citations\"]} == {\"instruction\", \"mechanism\", \"guard\"}\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/219/stub/tests/test_doctor.py:4:fail at the first commit and must fail: that is the definition of done,\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/records/158/counsel-04.json:46:    \"ruling_text\": \"verify-02 is correctly stopped as forge-unconcluded/tenant: the configured checks command returned no conclusions for 7a2529ee349547a259dbd8b0b78043b39bbf63c2, so verification returned before invoking an adapter; consequently, no verify-02.log is expected. Retry verification only after the forge exposes concluded, green checks for that exact SHA. This stop neither establishes a product defect nor changes the definition of done.\",\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/219/stub/tests/test_retry.py:3:reason that does not touch the definition of done is started again by a\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/records/158/counsel-06.json:46:    \"ruling_text\": \"verify-04 is correctly stopped as forge-unconcluded/tenant. For commit 43ca11c41030517d76757e4d7fea97a64478c88e, the tenant's configured forge-check command supplied no check conclusions, so verification returned before invoking the verifier; therefore no verify-04.log is expected. Retry verification after the forge exposes at least one concluded green check for that exact commit. If the read remains empty, repair the tenant command's GitHub authentication or API visibility. This stop neither establishes a product defect nor changes the definition of done.\",\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/records/158/counsel-07.json:46:    \"ruling_text\": \"verify-05 is a guarded forge-unconcluded/tenant stop, not a finding against item 158: after the bounded 600-second poll, the forge still reported the test check as in_progress, so verify correctly returned without calling the judging adapter. Once that check has concluded, rerun the mechanical verify/drive step; readiness and the definition of done remain unchanged.\",\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/records/194/counsel-01.json:46:    \"ruling_text\": \"verify-01 did not evaluate item 194: the configured Codex executor reported \\u201cSelected model is at capacity\\u201d and exited 1. The forge was read and all reported checks on 0ef9df110a325e26c701dac4d77af15f759e9b49 succeeded or were skipped, so no tenant check failure or change to the definition of done is presented. Retry verification when gpt-5.6-sol has capacity; the passing build remains the build to verify.\",\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/251/stub/attavo/cli.py:22:The doctor is the definition of done. It asserts five proofs against a\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attest/specs/251/stub/attavo/cli.py:235:(instruction, mechanism, guard, each a path and symbol or a ruling id),\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:47:from attavo.stops import RETRYABLE_CLASSES, counsel_record_for, signature_of\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:641:    instead of polling. again_by (R3) is the login that pressed `--again`, set\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:774:    the budget) starts on its own, and what `--again` starts by force\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:805:NEVER_RETRIED_CLASSES = (\"claims-boundary\", \"gate\", \"environment\")\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:816:def _signatures_match(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> bool:\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:833:    a plain pass nor `--again`'s own no-op read of the same fact; steer\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:843:def retry_state(root: Path, m: dict[str, Any], records_dir: Path, stage: str,\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:851:    \"answer\" -- and never when the class is one of NEVER_RETRIED_CLASSES,\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:854:    if stop_class is None or stop_class in NEVER_RETRIED_CLASSES or attempt is None:\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:861:    if stop_class in RETRYABLE_CLASSES:\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:889:    with no flag needed; `--again` is a no-op that says so when the plain\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:913:                print(\"attavo drive: --again inside the budget is a no-op; the pass retries \"\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:1006:    again_flag = \"--again\" in argv\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:1020:        print(\"attavo drive: --again requires --by LOGIN\", file=sys.stderr)\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:1023:        print(\"attavo drive: --by is only for --again\", file=sys.stderr)\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:1048:                  \"and --again\", file=sys.stderr)\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/cli.py:14:    attavo drive [N] [--until-ready] [--again --by LOGIN] [--report]\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/cli.py:343:--again --by LOGIN starts the next attempt of a stopped stage -- the\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:47:from attavo.stops import RETRYABLE_CLASSES, counsel_record_for, signature_of\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:641:    instead of polling. again_by (R3) is the login that pressed `--again`, set\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:774:    the budget) starts on its own, and what `--again` starts by force\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:805:NEVER_RETRIED_CLASSES = (\"claims-boundary\", \"gate\", \"environment\")\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:816:def _signatures_match(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> bool:\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:833:    a plain pass nor `--again`'s own no-op read of the same fact; steer\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:843:def retry_state(root: Path, m: dict[str, Any], records_dir: Path, stage: str,\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:851:    \"answer\" -- and never when the class is one of NEVER_RETRIED_CLASSES,\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:854:    if stop_class is None or stop_class in NEVER_RETRIED_CLASSES or attempt is None:\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:861:    if stop_class in RETRYABLE_CLASSES:\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:889:    with no flag needed; `--again` is a no-op that says so when the plain\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:913:                print(\"attavo drive: --again inside the budget is a no-op; the pass retries \"\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:1006:    again_flag = \"--again\" in argv\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:1020:        print(\"attavo drive: --again requires --by LOGIN\", file=sys.stderr)\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:1023:        print(\"attavo drive: --by is only for --again\", file=sys.stderr)\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py:1048:                  \"and --again\", file=sys.stderr)\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/stops.py:29:RETRYABLE_CLASSES = (\"died\", \"hung\", \"transient\", \"returned\")\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/report.py:87:        \"attavo drive N --again --by LOGIN: reset the budget and start the next attempt\"\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/doctor.py:213:    and proof five names it. again_by is the login that pressed `--again`\n/Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/doctor.py:284:def attempts_budget(m: dict[str, Any]) -> int:\n    return env\n\n\ndef _retry_base(root: Path, branch: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"The commit a retried record is committed on: origin's branch tip,\n    fetched now. It must contain this checkout's HEAD -- the stage's own\n    commits are in it, and a record's `sha` names them -- because the\n    driver neither merges nor rebases; a tip that does not is a refusal,\n    with the commits it would strand named by sha and author.\"\"\"\n    git(root, \"fetch\", \"-q\", \"origin\", branch)\n    tip = git(root, \"rev-parse\", \"FETCH_HEAD\").strip()\n    contained = subprocess.run([\"git\", \"-C\", str(root), \"merge-base\", \"--is-ancestor\", \"HEAD\", tip],\n                               capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)\n    if contained.returncode != 0:\n        ours = git(root, \"log\", \"--format=%h %an <%ae>\", f\"{tip}..HEAD\").splitlines()\n        raise TransportRefused(\n            f\"origin's {branch} moved under this checkout's own commits and the driver \"\n            f\"neither merges nor rebases them: {'; '.join(ours)}\")\n    return tip\n\n\n@contextlib.contextmanager\ndef record_worktree(root: Path, tip: str) -> Iterator[Path]:\n    \"\"\"A detached worktree of `tip` under the git dir, removed on exit:\n    the only place the driver commits. The item's checkout is the\n    executor's while a build runs, so nothing the driver writes or\n    commits, resets or merges is made there.\"\"\"\n    parent = stage_events_dir(root) / \"transport\"\n    parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    tree = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix=\"tx-\", dir=parent)) / \"head\"\n    git(root, \"worktree\", \"add\", \"--detach\", \"-q\", str(tree), tip)\n    try:\n        yield tree\n    finally:\n        try:\n            git(root, \"worktree\", \"remove\", \"--force\", str(tree))\n        except RuntimeError:\n            shutil.rmtree(tree, ignore_errors=True)\n            try:\n                git(root, \"worktree\", \"prune\")\n            except RuntimeError:\n                pass\n        shutil.rmtree(tree.parent, ignore_errors=True)\n\n\ndef _record_paths(tree: Path, record_paths: list[Path]) -> list[str]:\n    \"\"\"Each record's path relative to the tree, and each one's .log and\n    .filed, each if one exists (issues #89, #100): the paths `git commit\n    --only` names, so a transport commit never sweeps the index (issue\n    #46's regression, on the transport side).\"\"\"\n    paths: list[str] = []\n    for record_path in record_paths:\n        paths.append(str(record_path.relative_to(tree)))\n        for suffix in (\".log\", \".filed\"):\n            extra = record_path.with_suffix(suffix)\n            if extra.is_file():\n                paths.append(str(extra.relative_to(tree)))\n    return paths\n\n\ndef commit_record(tree: Path, record_paths: list[Path], message: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"`git commit --only` on the record paths given, in `tree` -- a\n    record worktree, never the checkout -- as driver_identity(), set by\n    the product and never the tenant's git config, so a driven item's\n    record commits are never indistinguishable from an operator's own.\n    Returns the commit's sha.\"\"\"\n    paths = _record_paths(tree, record_paths)\n    git(tree, \"add\", *paths)\n    git(tree, \"commit\", \"--only\", \"-q\", \"-m\", message, *paths, env=_driver_env())\n    return git(tree, \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\").strip()\n\n\ndef sync_checkout(root: Path, target: str) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Fast-forward the item's checkout to `target`, the one thing the\n    driver does to it. A pass in the same directory may hold the index\n    for a moment, so a lock is retried; a checkout already at or beyond\n    target is done.\"\"\"\n    err = \"\"\n    for _ in range(20):\n        run = subprocess.run([\"git\", \"-C\", str(root), \"merge\", \"--ff-only\", \"-q\", target],\n                             capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)\n        if run.returncode == 0:\n            return\n        err = run.stderr.strip()\n        contained = subprocess.run([\"git\", \"-C\", str(root), \"merge-base\", \"--is-ancestor\", target, \"HEAD\"],\n                                   capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)\n        if contained.returncode == 0:\n            return\n        time.sleep(0.1)\n    raise TransportRefused(f\"the checkout could not fast-forward to {target[:12]}: {err}\")\n\n\ndef _stage_copies(root: Path, paths: list[str]) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Stage the checkout's own untracked copies of records the driver\n    has just committed elsewhere, so the fast-forward that brings the\n    committed ones in finds them identical to the target and has nothing\n    to collide with -- and the record is never absent from the checkout,\n    which is what a pass or a reader beside the worker sees.\"\"\"\n    if not paths:\n        return\n    out = git(root, \"ls-files\", \"--others\", \"--exclude-standard\", \"--\", *paths).splitlines()\n    if out:\n        git(root, \"add\", \"--\", *out)\n\n\ndef transport_records(root: Path, branch: str, message: str,\n                      write: Callable[[Path], list[Path]],\n                      lease: bool = False) -> tuple[str, str | None]:\n    \"\"\"Commit records in a detached worktree of the branch's tip and push\n    them from there as HEAD:branch; then fast-forward the checkout.\n    `write(tree)` puts the records in the worktree and returns the record\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport json\nimport os\nimport shlex\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport tempfile\nimport time\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Any, cast\n\nfrom attavo.build import (\n    adapter_reports,\n    merge_adapter_config,\n    resolve_adapter,\n    split_adapter_stdout,\n)\nfrom attavo.common import MANIFEST, PRODUCT_ROOT, derive_item_id, git, now_iso, read_manifest\nfrom attavo.records import latest_attempt_path, latest_record, write_record\nfrom attavo.types import SpecRecord, StageRecord\nfrom attavo.verify import diff_since_approval, read_forge_checks\n\n# --- counsel: a stop is read inside the product first, before a person\n# (issue #100). Read-only -- it commits nothing and writes nothing outside\n# paths.records/<N>/ -- and it never acts: it proposes a ruling, a patch,\n# or names the one-line question a person must answer. ---\n\nCOUNSEL_STAGES = (\"build\", \"verify\")\n\n\ndef latest_non_pass_stage_record(records_dir: Path) -> tuple[str | None, StageRecord | None, Path | None]:\n    \"\"\"(stage, record, path) for the item's most recent non-pass build or\n    verify record, by its own \"at\" timestamp -- or, when neither stage\n    carries one, the item's latest refused spec-gate record (issue #133):\n    a refused gate is the same shape of stop, earlier, and it is read only\n    once a build or verify record exists to counsel first, since either\n    one already implies the gate that produced it passed. (None, None,\n    None) when none of the three exists. \"Most recent\" rather than\n    \"build, else verify\" so a stop on the stage that ran later (typically\n    verify, bound to a build that already passed) is the one counseled.\"\"\"\n    candidates: list[tuple[str, StageRecord, Path]] = []\n    for stage in COUNSEL_STAGES:\n        path = latest_attempt_path(records_dir, stage)\n        if path is None:\n            continue\n        # A path exists, so the record read from the same files does too.\n        rec = cast(StageRecord, latest_record(records_dir, stage))\n        if rec.get(\"status\") == \"pass\":\n            continue\n        candidates.append((stage, rec, path))\n    if candidates:\n        candidates.sort(key=lambda c: c[1].get(\"at\") or \"\")\n        return candidates[-1]\n    spec_path = latest_attempt_path(records_dir, \"spec\")\n    if spec_path is not None:\n        spec_rec = cast(SpecRecord, latest_record(records_dir, \"spec\"))\n        if spec_rec.get(\"status\") == \"refused\":\n            return \"spec\", spec_rec, spec_path\n    return None, None, None\n\n\ndef latest_stage_record(records_dir: Path) -> tuple[str | None, StageRecord | None, Path | None]:\n    \"\"\"(stage, record, path) for the item's most recent build or verify\n    record regardless of status, by its own \"at\" timestamp -- or, when\n    neither stage carries one, the item's latest spec-gate record,\n    regardless of status. The evidence set `attavo counsel --ask` reads\n    over (issue #175): a question is not bound to a stop, so the pass\n    filter latest_non_pass_stage_record applies does not apply here.\n    (None, None, None) when the item carries no record of any of the\n    three stages yet.\"\"\"\n    candidates: list[tuple[str, StageRecord, Path]] = []\n    for stage in COUNSEL_STAGES:\n        path = latest_attempt_path(records_dir, stage)\n        if path is None:\n            continue\n        candidates.append((stage, cast(StageRecord, latest_record(records_dir, stage)), path))\n    if candidates:\n        candidates.sort(key=lambda c: c[1].get(\"at\") or \"\")\n        return candidates[-1]\n    spec_path = latest_attempt_path(records_dir, \"spec\")\n    if spec_path is not None:\n        return \"spec\", cast(SpecRecord, latest_record(records_dir, \"spec\")), spec_path\n    return None, None, None\n\n\ndef counsel_forge_evidence(root: Path, forge_cfg: dict[str, Any], sha: str | None, tmp: Path\n                          ) -> tuple[dict[str, Any], Path]:\n    \"\"\"(forge, checks_path) -- issue #175. forge is the summary counsel's\n    own record carries: {\"read\", \"reason\", \"checks\"}, \"checks\" a list of\n    {\"name\", \"conclusion\"} pairs. checks_path is always written, under\n    tmp, as ATTAVO_COUNSEL_CHECKS: a JSON {\"read\", \"reason\", \"checks\"}\n    envelope whose own \"checks\" carry the richer {\"name\", \"conclusion\",\n    \"run_url\", \"log\"} shape the adapter reads -- \"log\" naming a sibling\n    file under tmp with that check's own failed-step log, fetched through\n    [forge].check_log_command for every check not \"success\". Absent\n    [forge].checks_command, absent [forge].check_log_command, or a\n    counseled record with no sha of its own: read is false and reason\n    names exactly which is missing -- never a checks list a reader could\n    mistake for \"all clear\" (an empty list from a real, successful read\n    is not this; that is read: true, checks: []).\"\"\"\n    checks_command = forge_cfg.get(\"checks_command\")\n    check_log_command = forge_cfg.get(\"check_log_command\")\n    checks_path = tmp / \"checks.json\"\n\n    if not checks_command:\n        reason = \"not read: no [forge].checks_command\"\n    elif not check_log_command:\n        reason = \"not read: no [forge].check_log_command\"\n    elif not sha:\n        reason = \"not read: the counseled record carries no sha\"\n    else:\n        reason = None\n\n    if reason is not None:\n        envelope = {\"read\": False, \"reason\": reason, \"checks\": []}\n        checks_path.write_text(json.dumps(envelope, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n        return envelope, checks_path\n\n    # reason is None only when checks_command, check_log_command, and sha are all set.\n    checks_command, sha = cast(str, checks_command), cast(str, sha)\n    raw_checks, _ok = read_forge_checks(root, checks_command, sha)\n    payload_checks: list[dict[str, Any]] = []\n    record_checks: list[dict[str, Any]] = []\n    for i, c in enumerate(raw_checks[\"conclusions\"]):\n        name, conclusion = c[\"name\"], c[\"conclusion\"]\n        log_path = None\n        if conclusion != \"success\":\n            log_run = subprocess.run(\n                f\"{check_log_command} {shlex.quote(sha)} {shlex.quote(name)}\",\n                shell=True, cwd=str(root), capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)\n            if log_run.returncode == 0 and log_run.stdout.strip():\n                log_file = tmp / f\"check-{i:02d}.log\"\n                log_file.write_text(log_run.stdout)\n                log_path = str(log_file)\n        payload_checks.append({\"name\": name, \"conclusion\": conclusion,\n                               \"run_url\": None, \"log\": log_path})\n        record_checks.append({\"name\": name, \"conclusion\": conclusion})\n\n    checks_path.write_text(json.dumps(\n        {\"read\": True, \"reason\": None, \"checks\": payload_checks}, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n    return {\"read\": True, \"reason\": None, \"checks\": record_checks}, checks_path\n\n\ndef counsel_graph_text(root: Path, m: dict[str, Any], item_id: str, sha: str | None,\n                       checks_command: str | None) -> str:\n    \"\"\"A text report of the item's own place on the branch (issue #175):\n    the default branch's first-parent log since the item's own branch\n    point, this branch's own commits (sha, author, subject), the markers\n    and records present at the counseled sha, and the check runs on the\n    last three shas on this branch -- so a stop whose cause is \"the\n    checks are on the record commit, not the code commit\" is readable\n    without a terminal. Best-effort: a git read that fails (a shallow\n    checkout, an unset refs/remotes/origin/HEAD) is noted in the text\n    rather than raised, since the graph is evidence, not a gate.\"\"\"\n    records_rel = m[\"paths\"][\"records\"]\n    parts: list[str] = []\n\n    symref = subprocess.run(\n        [\"git\", \"-C\", str(root), \"symbolic-ref\", \"-q\", \"refs/remotes/origin/HEAD\"],\n        capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)\n    default_ref = symref.stdout.strip() if symref.returncode == 0 else None\n\n    if default_ref:\n        try:\n            branch_point = git(root, \"merge-base\", default_ref, \"HEAD\").strip()\n            parts.append(\n                \"## the default branch, first-parent, since this item's branch point\\n\\n\" +\n                (git(root, \"log\", \"--first-parent\", \"--oneline\",\n                    f\"{branch_point}..{default_ref}\").strip() or \"(nothing new)\"))\n            parts.append(\n                \"## this branch's own commits since the branch point\\n\\n\" +\n                (git(root, \"log\", \"--first-parent\", \"--format=%H %an %s\",\n                    f\"{branch_point}..HEAD\").strip() or \"(no commits yet)\"))\n        except RuntimeError as exc:\n            parts.append(f\"## the default branch\\n\\n(could not be read: {exc})\")\n    else:\n        parts.append(\"## the default branch\\n\\n(refs/remotes/origin/HEAD is not set)\")\n\n    if sha:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,180p' /Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/adapters/claude-code/README.md; rg -n \\\"def transient_detail|transient_detail|authentication_failed|capacity|rate_limit\\\" /Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/adapters/claude-code/run; sed -n '800,880p' /Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py; sed -n '760,825p' /Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py; sed -n '330,360p' /Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/cli.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,180p' /Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/adapters/claude-code/README.md; rg -n \\\"def transient_detail|transient_detail|authentication_failed|capacity|rate_limit\\\" /Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/adapters/claude-code/run; sed -n '800,880p' /Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py; sed -n '760,825p' /Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/drive.py; sed -n '330,360p' /Users/davidbean/Projects/attest/attavo/cli.py\"","aggregated_output":"# adapters/claude-code\n\nRuns `claude -p` against the spec. Configured through `[executor.claude-code]`\nin the tenant's manifest (see `../README.md` for how that table reaches the\nadapter as `ATTAVO_ADAPTER_CONFIG`, and lands verbatim in `build-NN.json` as\n`adapter_config`). **Never put a secret in this table** — it lands in a\nrecord.\n\n## Keys\n\n- `auth` — `\"api\"` (default) or `\"subscription\"`.\n  - `\"api\"`: runs with `--bare`. Requires `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`; usage bills per token.\n  - `\"subscription\"`: omits `--bare`; adds `--setting-sources user` and `--strict-mcp-config` so the tenant's project hooks and MCP servers do not load (`CLAUDE.md` still does). Runs on the operator's subscription login; usage is included in the plan. `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is not required.\n- `model` — omitted means the executor's default. When set, passed as `--model`, and trusted verbatim as the record's `model` rather than picked out of the tool's own usage report (issue #77).\n- `setting_sources` — default `\"user\"`. Only meaningful in subscription mode; ignored in api mode (`--bare` already excludes everything).\n- `permission_mode` — default `\"acceptEdits\"`.\n- `allowed_tools` is retired (issue #78): a manifest that still declares `[executor.claude-code].allowed_tools` is refused by name rather than silently accepted or ignored. See below.\n\n## The tenant's tests\n\n`attavo build` runs the tenant's gates after each commit and feeds a\nfailure back on the next iteration — the executor is not the runner of the\ntenant's checks, and is not told what they are. When `ATTAVO_TEST_COMMAND`\nis set (`../README.md`), the instruction gives it: \"to run tests while you\nwork, run `<command>`: it runs the tests selected for the files you have\nchanged and stops at `<N>` s. Do not run the whole test suite or any\nwhole-tree check yourself: after you commit, the gates are run for you and\nany failure is returned to you on the next iteration.\" The \"stops at\" clause\nis present only when `ATTAVO_TEST_TIMEOUT_S` is set. When\n`ATTAVO_TEST_COMMAND` is unset, the instruction says nothing about tests.\n\n## The sandbox is the boundary, not an allowed_tools list\n\nEvery invocation carries `--settings` with a `sandbox` object\n(`sandbox.enabled: true`, `allowUnsandboxedCommands: false`,\n`network.allowedDomains: []`) — confirmed live against `claude 2.1.268`:\nthe sandbox refuses a write outside the working directory and any\noutbound connection, at the OS level, before `--allowedTools` is ever\nconsulted. Verify's `--settings` additionally sets\n`filesystem.denyWrite` on the working directory itself, so \"make no\nedits, write no files\" is mechanical rather than merely instructed.\n`--allowedTools` is therefore fixed per stage to the tool names the\nsandbox does not itself govern — `Read,Edit,Write,Bash` for build,\n`Read,Bash` for verify — not a curated `Bash(git *)`-style prefix list a\ntenant used to grow one denial at a time; `[executor.claude-code]` no\nlonger has a key for it. The network stays closed for both stages; an\nexecutor that needs a package index is a tenant fact for a later\nmanifest key, not this one.\n\nThe raw result's `permission_denials` (in `build-NN.log` since `v0.3.4`)\nstill exists, but a Bash tool call the sandbox will refuse at the OS\nlevel is never denied at the permission layer in the first place — a\nrefusal there shows up as the underlying shell command's own non-zero\nexit in the transcript, not as a `permission_denials` entry.\n\n## cost_usd is a meter, not always a bill\n\n`build-NN.json`'s `cost_usd` is this adapter's report of the tool's\nlist-price meter. It is billed only when `adapter_config.auth` (this\ntable's `auth` key) is `\"api\"`. Under `auth = \"subscription\"`, the same\nfigure is a measure of usage against the operator's plan, not a charge —\nthat build ran on the subscription login, not an API key.\n\n## The raw result\n\nThe whole `--output-format json` result is printed to stdout before this\nadapter's own final line, so `attavo build` saves it verbatim as the\ntranscript in `build-NN.log` — the tool's own account of what happened,\nincluding fields (`num_turns`, `duration_ms`, `is_error`,\n`permission_denials`) the final line never carries. `is_error: true`, no\n`structured_output` at all, or a `status: \"question\"` whose `question` is\nmissing or under 40 characters all make the adapter exit 1 (build records\nthe attempt `failed`) instead of a halt someone has to rule on — a\nquestion worth a person's judgment is at least a sentence.\n\n## A report is not a question (issue #88)\n\nA `status: \"question\"` whose text clears the 40-character gate but\ncarries no `\"?\"` is a report, not a question — five of tenant zero's\ntwelve non-pass build records were exactly this (\"I've traced the\nspec's 5 criteria against the tree and dispatched an implementation\nagent…\"), each costing a person a read and a re-launch for nothing to\nrule on. This adapter re-invokes `claude` once more on a report,\nappending a line asking it to either continue to done or ask a real\nquestion, before ever reporting a halt. A second report in a row still\nhalts, but its report line carries `\"class\": \"non-question\"` and both\nraw results already reached the transcript above it — see `../README.md`.\n`tokens` and `cost_usd` on that line sum both calls; `model`,\n`model_version`, and `adapter_version` come from the second call only.\n\n## Work that can outlive the invocation (issue #88)\n\nConfirmed live against `claude 2.1.269`: a prompt encouraging a\nsubagent produced `subagent_stats.spawned: 1` when the Agent tool was\nallowed, and `0` with `\"Agent\"` alone in `--disallowedTools` — the\ntool's own reply named `Workflow` as the other path to background work,\nso both are disallowed by name on every invocation, both stages.\nTenant zero's own specimen (`#659` build-01) spawned three with neither\ntool in `--allowedTools` at all, so the flag is checked after the fact\ntoo: any response whose `subagent_stats.spawned` is not zero is refused\nby name, with the counts, rather than accepted as `done` — a build the\ntool's own background work could still be writing to.\n737:# R3: a vendor's capacity, rate, or 5xx refusal is transient -- classed on\n742:TRANSIENT_MARKERS = (\"overloaded\", \"rate limit\", \"rate_limit\", \"429\", \"500\", \"502\", \"503\", \"529\",\n746:def transient_detail(response: dict) -> str | None:\n1077:        detail = transient_detail(response)\n# tuple -- counsel reads it exactly like any other non-pass record, and\n# an \"answer\" verdict starts the next attempt steered by it, the same as\n# any class counsel can rule on; a question nobody answers (no counsel\n# configured, or its own verdict is \"person\") simply never becomes\n# eligible, with no exclusion needed to keep it that way.\nNEVER_RETRIED_CLASSES = (\"claims-boundary\", \"gate\", \"environment\")\n\n\ndef _budget_stage(stage: str, stop_class: str | None) -> str:\n    \"\"\"The stage whose [drive].attempts budget a stop spends -- build for\n    a returned review (the check rejection or the blocking finding is the\n    build's to answer, never the review's own to repeat), the stop's own\n    stage for everything else.\"\"\"\n    return \"build\" if stop_class == \"returned\" else stage\n\n\ndef _signatures_match(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Whether the last two attempts of stage carry the identical,\n    non-null signature -- the early stop (R3): two attempts of one stage\n    ending the same way spend no third.\"\"\"\n    attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, stage)\n    if len(attempts) < 2:\n        return False\n    nums = sorted(attempts)\n    sig_a = (attempts[nums[-1]].get(\"stop\") or {}).get(\"signature\")\n    sig_b = (attempts[nums[-2]].get(\"stop\") or {}).get(\"signature\")\n    return sig_a is not None and sig_a == sig_b\n\n\nclass RetryState(NamedTuple):\n    \"\"\"Whether a plain pass, right now, would start the next attempt of a\n    stopped stage (R3) -- eligible; a transient stop still short of\n    [drive].retry_after_s carries waiting_s instead, eligible for neither\n    a plain pass nor `--again`'s own no-op read of the same fact; steer\n    names the counsel record an \"answer\" verdict would hand the next\n    attempt; target_stage is the stage the next attempt actually starts\n    (build, for a returned review).\"\"\"\n    eligible: bool\n    waiting_s: float | None\n    steer: str | None\n    target_stage: str\n\n\ndef retry_state(root: Path, m: dict[str, Any], records_dir: Path, stage: str,\n                stop_class: str | None, attempt: int | None) -> RetryState:\n    \"\"\"The one read behind both the report's own kind/says (a stopped\n    stage that would retry reads as \"acts\" or \"waits\", never \"yours\") and\n    the pass's own act, so the two cannot say different things about the\n    same stop. died, hung, transient, and returned retry with no counsel\n    asked, inside the shared budget and past the early stop; every other\n    class retries only when counsel's own verdict for this exact stop is\n    \"answer\" -- and never when the class is one of NEVER_RETRIED_CLASSES,\n    whatever counsel might have said.\"\"\"\n    target_stage = _budget_stage(stage, stop_class)\n    if stop_class is None or stop_class in NEVER_RETRIED_CLASSES or attempt is None:\n        return RetryState(False, None, None, target_stage)\n    spent = next_attempt_number(records_dir, target_stage) - 1\n    if spent >= attempts_budget(m):\n        return RetryState(False, None, None, target_stage)\n    if _signatures_match(records_dir, stage):\n        return RetryState(False, None, None, target_stage)\n    if stop_class in RETRYABLE_CLASSES:\n        if stop_class == \"transient\":\n            attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, stage)\n            rec = attempts.get(attempt)\n            age = _seconds_since(rec.get(\"at\")) if rec else None\n            wait = retry_after_s(m)\n            if age is not None and age < wait:\n                return RetryState(False, wait - age, None, target_stage)\n        return RetryState(True, None, None, target_stage)\n    counsel = counsel_record_for(records_dir, stage, attempt)\n    if counsel is not None and counsel.get(\"verdict\") == \"answer\":\n        target = f\"{stage}-{attempt:02d}\"\n        counsel_attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, \"counsel\")\n        matches = [n for n, rec in counsel_attempts.items() if rec.get(\"record\") == target]\n        if matches:\n            return RetryState(True, None, f\"counsel-{max(matches):02d}\", target_stage)\n    return RetryState(False, None, None, target_stage)\n\n\ndef _pass_once(root: Path, m: dict[str, Any], item_id: str, again: str | None) -> tuple[int, \"DriveReport\"]:\n        return 1\n    if outcome != \"pushed\":\n        print(f\"attavo drive: stopped: could not push the {cls} record: {err}\")\n        return 1\n    if not has_origin(root):\n        print(f\"attavo drive: note: no origin remote; {stage}-{nn:02d}.json is committed, not pushed\")\n    print(f\"attavo drive: recovered {marker.stem} as {cls}: {detail}\")\n    return 0\n\n\ndef _advance_again(root: Path, m: dict[str, Any], item_id: str, stage: str, head: str | None,\n                   again_by: str | None = None) -> int:\n    \"\"\"The next attempt of a stopped stage, bypassing the level a plain\n    pass leaves alone for the operator: what a plain retry (R3, inside\n    the budget) starts on its own, and what `--again` starts by force\n    once the budget is spent or an early stop fired. A review reads the\n    forge fresh here -- once, since the report's own stopped reading\n    never touched it -- rather than reusing a read that was never taken.\"\"\"\n    if stage == \"build\":\n        return _start_stage(root, m, item_id, \"build\", None, again_by=again_by)\n    checks_command = (m.get(\"forge\") or {}).get(\"checks_command\")\n    if not checks_command:\n        print(\"attavo drive: refused: no [forge].checks_command in the manifest\")\n        return 1\n    forge_checks, ok = read_forge_checks(root, checks_command, cast(str, head))\n    if not ok:\n        conclusions = forge_checks[\"conclusions\"]\n        named = \", \".join(f\"{c['name']} {c['conclusion']}\" for c in conclusions) or \"no checks reported\"\n        print(f\"attavo drive: forge checks not green on {cast(str, head)[:12]}: {named}\")\n        return 0\n    return _start_stage(root, m, item_id, \"verify\", forge_checks, again_by=again_by)\n\n\n# --- R3: the retry decision, shared by the report's own kind/says and the\n# pass's own act (retry_state) so the two cannot drift. ---\n\n# Never retried by this item, whatever counsel might rule: a\n# claims-boundary stop is a person's; a tenant's gate refusal is the\n# spec-gate's own, earlier than any of this; an environment refusal is\n# recorded here and acted on by R4's breaker. A question is not in this\n# tuple -- counsel reads it exactly like any other non-pass record, and\n# an \"answer\" verdict starts the next attempt steered by it, the same as\n# any class counsel can rule on; a question nobody answers (no counsel\n# configured, or its own verdict is \"person\") simply never becomes\n# eligible, with no exclusion needed to keep it that way.\nNEVER_RETRIED_CLASSES = (\"claims-boundary\", \"gate\", \"environment\")\n\n\ndef _budget_stage(stage: str, stop_class: str | None) -> str:\n    \"\"\"The stage whose [drive].attempts budget a stop spends -- build for\n    a returned review (the check rejection or the blocking finding is the\n    build's to answer, never the review's own to repeat), the stop's own\n    stage for everything else.\"\"\"\n    return \"build\" if stop_class == \"returned\" else stage\n\n\ndef _signatures_match(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Whether the last two attempts of stage carry the identical,\n    non-null signature -- the early stop (R3): two attempts of one stage\n    ending the same way spend no third.\"\"\"\n    attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, stage)\n    if len(attempts) < 2:\n        return False\n    nums = sorted(attempts)\n    sig_a = (attempts[nums[-1]].get(\"stop\") or {}).get(\"signature\")\n    sig_b = (attempts[nums[-2]].get(\"stop\") or {}).get(\"signature\")\nrecord already names the commit's sha. On a review, ATTAVO_FORGE_CHECKS\nnames the file the pass already wrote the forge's own read into (the\nchecks it saw on the head before its marker, reconciler ruling B); verify\nreads that file instead of polling when it is set.\n\n--until-ready repeats the pass in this same process, sleeping\nATTAVO_DRIVE_POLL_INTERVAL_S between passes, until the level is the\noperator's or done (exit 0), a concluded check reads red (exit 1), or an\nunconcluded one has not concluded within ATTAVO_DRIVE_POLL_BOUND_S (exit\n1) -- a stage running in its own worker is not bounded by either\nconstant, only time spent waiting on the forge is. Without the flag,\ndrive is one pass, then stop.\n\n--again --by LOGIN starts the next attempt of a stopped stage -- the\noperator's own resume, until a later reconciler ruling drives a retry\nitself; --by names who pressed it, as approve's does, and the marker\ncarries it. A stage's liveness is read from the worker's heartbeat, the\nfile the process holding it writes, never from a pid someone else\nrecorded. --recover\nis gone: `attavo drive --recover` now exits 2 saying so; recovery is\nwhat a pass does on its own, the moment the report owes one.\n\n--all runs one pass, in its own process, in every ../attavo-worktree-N\nbeside this checkout -- each line either prints is prefixed with the\nworktree's own directory name.\n\n--report prints, as JSON against schema/drive-report.schema.json, what\nitem N is waiting on: its state, whose move it is (yours, waits, acts,\ndone), and one sentence in the operator's words. It reads exactly what a\npass reads -- the item's records, its invocation markers and heartbeats,\none read of the forge -- and does nothing else: it writes no record,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
